  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">IOM Ukraine Crisis Response Plan 2023</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Starting on 24 February 2022, a large-scale invasion of Ukraine by the Russian Federation triggered an unprecedented humanitarian crisis across the country. A year and a half into the war in Ukraine, the humanitarian needs are still as present as an estimated 17.6 million people remain in need of immediate assistance (OCHA Ukraine Humanitarian Needs Overview). This includes over 5.1 million people who continue to be displaced as of the April 2023 (IOM Displacement Tracking Matrix General Population Round June 2023).  The upcoming winter season further increases the conflict-affected population&#x2019;s vulnerabilities with temperatures expected to drop as low as -20 degrees Celsius. 
 
Through this project, IOM is delivering winterization assistance through a multi-sectoral approach focusing on Multi-Purpose Cash Assistance (MCPA), health, critical infrastructure repairs, protection and overall enhancing response to shocks.</narrative>
  </description>
